tanbridge house secondary school in west  sussex  open only to vulnerable pupils and  children of key workers  it's much busier than during the first  lockdown with around 100 students  attending  last monday a day after boris johnson  said that schools were safe he ordered  them to close  and also announced that exams would be  cancelled the head teacher jules white  it's been a constant battle to keep up  it's been very difficult i think  everyone and every head teacher has  sympathy for the government and the  department for education  in these times of a pandemic but the  mixed messaging the inconsistent  messaging  change after change you tune after  u-turn has been very very difficult  to deal with head teachers we really  know what we're doing  we want our schools to be open where we  can we want to look after vulnerable  children we want to allow the parents of  critical workers you know they need to  work as well  but the goal posts they think to change  every five minutes we waste  time resource and energy it's so  difficult to manage and most of all  children and parents are not getting a  consistent message either  i mean i guess if you have more notice  then you could have invested more in  online learning made sure that the  laptops were there  made sure that the teachers were  equipped to provide it and as a result  you're having to scramble that's  absolutely right if you look at just  before christmas there was  the threat of legal action about schools  who were thinking about closing because  of public  health risks then over the christmas  myself and other head teachers  we really busted a gut to try and put in  place  math testing plans which were pretty  flawed in the first place  then just before we go back we then have  to  change and move towards children not  coming in and online learning  now at the moment just in the the last  week the secretary of state has said  we're going to trust teachers on exam  grades but at the same time  talks about having to use ofsted to come  and check out what we're doing is really  just  bewildering and almost impossible to  manage we know  our children we know our families and if  we're actually trusted to deliver  we will do we we really understand that  there is a crisis upon us  and we have to do everything we can to  make sure our schools are open  when it's safe to do so another big call  by the government this week was the  decision to cancel  exams i was quite interested in the  survey results it shows that  overwhelmingly people think the teachers  think that it's right to cancel exams  but actually some comprehensive  contingency plan should be in place  already so we should know what the  replacement assessment should be and  does that surprise you  we've been asking for contingency plans  and a plan  b for some time even before this latest  variant came into place there was a real  risk that children were missing too much  time in school to have a fair go at a  national exam  program yet here we are no plans have  been  announced going to consultation again we  all feel that this should have been in  place  it is a difficult problem i think at the  root of things  teachers will have to be trusted to give  fair grades  for all of the exams hopefully there can  be some sort of external  checking or moderation that may be a way  forward to give further confidence to  the system  but again we need to be consulted with  we need to be listened to  we actually know what we're doing and we  really hope that dfe and off well take  note of that and we avoid the crisis and  the fiasco of last summer i think is  everyone wants to trust teachers of  course but  at the same time you do you can't help  but think that if teachers have complete  control over what grades are given  we're going to see some pretty high  grades been given out  yes i think last year we saw some great  inflation i think we have to be  realistic about that i also think we  have to be realistic that  no system is going to be perfect we're  in the middle of a pandemic but teachers  will use proper professional judgment  perhaps we can use some form of  online testing or a window of external  assessment which is  fair to all students much more reduced  that might be a reference point  again we will do our absolute best to  treat every child  fairly and we don't actually want to  just give away  grades that's not what we're in the job  for we want to make sure that every  child whether they're in search of a  college place  a university place an apprenticeship is  given  the opportunity to do so in a fair way  a survey of a thousand head teachers  carried out by campaign group worth less  and shared with sophie ridge on sunday  found that 52 percent have  no confidence in the department for  education's decision-making  and 41 percent have hardly any only six  percent  are fairly confident and just one  percent feel confident  91 percent of head teachers said that  the government was right to cancel all  assessments for summer 2021  only 9 said that was the wrong decision  but 92 percent of heads said that  comprehensive contingency plans for  alternative grading systems should  already have been set out by the  department for education  simon liebers head of the exam's body of  course has been in the job just one week  quite a baptism of fire there is a plan  b um  my my my officials and the department  spent quite a lot of time  last year working that out in detail but  i think one's got to recognize that  there's a it's a sort of framework in a  sense because  plan b as it's implemented in january is  not going to be the same as plan b if  it's implemented because because exams  are  cancelled in maine and i think the other  thing to recognize is that at the end of  the day  um people need to feel that the results  are fair we need to have a proper  opportunity to consult with parents with  students with teachers with schools or  school organizations  how can you make sure whatever replaces  the exams  is fair and doesn't discriminate against  people from  less advantaged backgrounds um you would  be idle to pretend that's a  straightforward  um challenge clearly any sort of  arrangements do need to take account of  the fact that we've got a very wide  range of learning experiences that have  to be assessed and evaluated  i think there's going to be a big  challenge in terms of making sure that  the  um the the teacher assessed grades the  um the methods we use encompass that  range of learning but at the same time i  think they have to reflect  actual achievement and actual learning  some people have had an absolutely  appalling experience and lost masses of  learning  others have virtually been unimpacted  have you got any  ideas for how to make a fair assessment  system  it's already been been made clear that  the the system is going to be based on  teacher assessment the question then is  how you  make sure that teachers assess teacher  assessment is relatively consistent  across  the um the school network you've got to  deal with the reality that there are  perverse incentives there's quite a big  grade inflation last year there's lots  of potential for an even bigger grade  inflation  this year the reality is that exams  aren't always fair some pupils will have  had better education than others  some children will have personal  circumstances that mean they do less  well at school  but like with so many other things the  pandemic is taking  existing problems and exacerbating them  the prime minister hopes to reopen  schools after the february half term  that is now looking optimistic on  current evidence  2021 looks to be just as disruptive as  the year before  my piece on schools there well in  response the department for education  has sent  us a statement and this is it we know  this is a challenging time for schools  and pupils and that's why we're  launching a consultation with offcoal  next week  on the best options for alternatives to  exams to ensure  every young person gets a fair grade for  their work grades will be based on  teacher assessment and support will be  provided to ensure grades are awarded  fairly and  consistently every major public health  decision we've taken has been guided  by the best scientific and medical  advocate advice and we are working to  ensure  the best possible outcome for schools  and pupils
 

