t  no system is going to be perfect we're  in the middle of a pandemic but teachers  will use proper professional judgment  perhaps we can use some form of  online testing or a window of external  assessment which is  fair to all students much more reduced  that might be a reference point  again we will do our absolute best to  treat every child  fairly and we don't actually want to  just give away  grades that's not what we're in the job  for we want to make sure that every  child whether they're in search of a  college place  a university place an apprenticeship is  given  the opportunity to do so in a fair way  a survey of a thousand head teachers  carried out by campaign group worth less  and shared with sophie ridge on sunday  found that 52 percent have  no confidence in the department for  education's decision-making  and 41 percent have hardly any only six  percent  are fairly confident and just one  percent feel confident  91 percent of head teachers said that  the government was right to cancel all  assessments for summer 2021  only 9 said that was the wrong decision  but 92 percent of heads said that  comprehensive contingency plans for  alternative grading systems should  already have been set out by the  department for education  simon liebers head of the exam's body of  course has been in the job just one week  quite a baptism of fire there is a plan  b um  my my my officials and the department  spent quite a lot of time  last year working that out in detail but  i think one's got to recognize that  there's a it's a sort of framework in a  sense because  plan b as it's implemented in january is  not going to be the same as plan b if  it's implemented because because exams  are  cancelled in maine and i think the other  thing to recognize is that at the end of  the day  um people need to feel that the results  are fair we need to have a proper  opportunity to consult with parents with  students with teachers with schools or  school organizations  how can you make sure whatever replaces  the exams  is fair and doesn't discriminate against  people from  less advantaged backgrounds um you would  be idle to pretend that's a  straightforward  um challenge clearly any sort of  arrangements do need to take account of  the fact that we've got a very wide  range of learning experiences that have  to be assessed and evaluated  i think there's going to be a big  challenge in terms of making sure that  the  um the the teacher assessed grades the  um the methods we use encompass that  range of learning but at the same time i  think they have to reflect  actual achievement and actual learning  some people have had an absolutely  appalling experience and lost masses of  learning  others have virtually been unimpacted  have you got any  ideas for how to make a fair assessment  system  it's already been been made clear that  the the system is going to be based on  teacher assessment the question then is  how you  make sure that teachers assess teacher  assessment is relatively consistent  across  the um the school network you've got to  deal with the reality that there are  perverse incentives there's quite a big  grade inflation last year there's lots  of potential for an even bigger grade  inflation  this year the reality is that exams  aren't always fair some pupils will have  had better education than others  some children will have personal  circumstances that mean they do less  well at school  but like with so many other things the  pandemic is taking  existing problems and exacerbating them  the prime minister hopes to reopen  schools after the february half term  that is now looking optimistic on  current evidence  2021 looks to be just as disruptive as  the year before  my piece on schools there well in  response the department for education  has sent  us a statement and this is it we know  this is a challenging time for schools  and pupils and that's why we're  launching a consultation with offcoal  next week  on the best options for alternatives to  exams to ensure  every young person gets a fair grade for  their work grades will be based on  teacher assessment and support will be  provided to ensure grades are awarded  fairly and  consistently every major public health  decision we've taken has been guided  by the best scientific and medical  advocate advice and we are working to  ensure  the best possible outcome for schools  and pupils


